  "full_response": "### **MapReduce Programming Model**\n**MapReduce** is a programming model and processing framework designed for **large-scale data processing** across distributed clusters. It was popularized by **Google** in 2004 and later implemented in open-source systems like **Apache Hadoop**. The model simplifies parallel computation by breaking tasks into two main phases: **Map** and **Reduce**.\n\n---\n\n## **Core Concepts of MapReduce**\nThe model consists of three key stages:\n\n### **1. Map Phase**\n- **Input:** A set of key-value pairs (`<k1, v1>`).\n- **Processing:** A **Mapper** function processes each input pair and emits intermediate key-value pairs (`<k2, v2>`).\n- **Example:**\n  - **Input:** `<\"doc1\", \"hello world hello\">`\n  - **Mapper Output:** `<\"hello\", 1>`, `<\"world\", 1>`, `<\"hello\", 1>`\n\n### **2. Shuffle & Sort Phase (Handled Automatically)**\n- The system **groups** all intermediate values (`v2`) by their keys (`k2`).\n- **Example:**\n  - After shuffling: `<\"hello\", [1, 1]>`, `<\"world\", [1]>`\n\n### **3. Reduce Phase**\n- **Input:** A key (`k2`) and a list of associated values (`[v2]`).\n- **Processing:** A **Reducer** function aggregates these values and produces a final output (`<k3, v3>`).\n- **Example:**\n  - **Reducer Input:** `<\"hello\", [1, 1]>`\n  - **Reducer Output:** `<\"hello\", 2>`\n\n---\n\n## **How MapReduce Works in Big Data Processing**\n1. **Data Splitting:**\n   - Input data is divided into **fixed-size chunks** (e.g., 64MB or 128MB blocks in Hadoop).\n   - Each chunk is processed independently by a **Mapper** task.\n\n2. **Parallel Execution:**\n   - **Mappers** run in parallel across multiple nodes in a cluster.\n   - **Reducers** collect and aggregate results from Mappers.\n\n3. **Fault Tolerance:**\n   - If a node fails, the system **reassigns** its tasks to another node.\n   - **Speculative execution** prevents stragglers (slow nodes) from delaying the job.\n\n4. **Scalability",
